{"kind":"assert","text":"STOP under cb95cb26 condition 1, for the cutover head (05d618e2). ARCHIVE_BOUNDS.attempts (512 per pass) cannot hold the worst-case entry with one birth copy per foreign sequence. I changed no cap.\n\nAt head d02f9bf8, the birth archive path works end to end:\n- derive plans foreign-birth.\n- The scope scans, inventories and supplies birth copies through retained_birth.\n- git writes and reads journal/v1/retained/birth/\u003cgenesis\u003e in v1 and v2, and the exact-use check stays.\n- git passes 240 of 240.\n- The archive-foreign tests that the birth work broke now pass with birth costs counted.\n\nAttempt arithmetic, from the charge sites (summed, not run as a 128-use fixture):\n- A direct use pays 1 attempt at each of four sites: the #copy request (archive-foreign.ts:86), the inventory use check (archive-inventory.ts:107), the reader request (archive-retained.ts:113), and the add() lookup (archive-inventory.ts:139).\n- A birth pays 1 at three sites: #copy, the reader request and add(). Its retained_birth lookup is one native call and no attempt.\n- Worst case, 128 later uses from 128 sequences: 4 x 128 = 512 attempts before births, plus at least the root definition read. That is already over 512. With births it is 512 + 3 x 128 = 896.\n- So the worst-case entry is not archivable in one pass today, and births do not create that condition. They lower the ceiling: with a base of B attempts, the most distinct later-use sequences per entry falls from about (512 - B) / 4 to about (512 - B) / 7.\n- inputWork (64 MiB) also cannot hold the pre-birth worst case: 128 copies of 256 KiB at about 33 units per byte is about 1.1 G. Births add up to the same again.\n- Copy objects per entry go from at most 128 to at most 256. That stays under the scout layout's 512 and the selection's 1024, but it shares the 512 with other objects.\n\nChoose one:\n(a) Accept that births lower the per-entry archive ceiling. The note states the old and new ceilings and the attempt sum per use and per birth. No cap changes.\n(b) Drop a birth's add() lookup and reader-request attempts as repeats of work the scan already paid. A birth then costs 1 attempt, and the ceiling becomes about (512 - B) / 5.\n\nI recommend (a). The worst case already exceeds the cap before births. (b) changes the cost accounting at two shared sites, and that change belongs in its own review, not inside the cutover.\n\nA second fact. On the cutover branch, 65 scope archive* tests fail both before and after the birth work: archive-driver, archive-installed, archive-cadence, archive-host, archive-input-work and others. Main passes them. They are cutover-branch failures, so the lane classifies and fixes them before the gate, as it did with derive (22), platform (21) and replay (7)."}